event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_07dbaadda6e34b75006a5fd679f6888196b04643d574498116","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":314,"text":"The latest notable news is that NASA’s **Perseverance Mars rover has been studying “Broom Point,”** a layered bedrock unit on the rim of Jezero Crater that appears to preserve a record of **repeated ancient asteroid impacts**. NASA says the rover found evidence that a roughly **245-foot / 75-meter-thick** stack of rock there was built by multiple impact events, and the rocks may be **more than 3.9 billion years old**【5:0†source】.\n\nNASA also released new imagery from Perseverance, including a **sweeping Mastcam-Z view of Broom Point** looking back toward the outside lip of Jezero Crater’s rim, plus an orbital map showing the rover’s route to the site【5:2†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a **full marathon — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — on Mars**. It reached that mark on its **1,890th Martian day**, about **five years and four months** into the mission, much faster than Opportunity reached the same distance【5:1†source】. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured Perseverance from orbit shortly before the milestone, showing the rover and its tracks on the Martian surface【5:0†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still healthy and productive, has passed a major driving milestone, and is now investigating very ancient rocks beyond Jezero Crater that could reveal more about Mars’ early impact history."}

